Chance Trade_elasticity
Title: Trade elasticity
Description: The elasticity describes how much (in percent) exports or imports will change per 1% change in price.~
~
If you are have information to support it, you can vary this by sector, and even by trade_direction, if you think there is an asymmetry between exports and imports. ~
~
A positive elasticity means that for either imports or exports, then the imports or exports respectively decreases with an increase in price. For imports, the reference price is the price paid by a US consumer to purchase the imported good. For exports, the price is the price the good is sold at on the open market.
Definition: {LogNormal(1.8, 1.5, over:sector)}~
LogNormal( Elasticity_means_by_, 1.5, over:Sector, over:Trade_direction)
NodeLocation: 248,440,1
NodeSize: 56,32
WindState: 2,108,176,720,533
ValueState: 2,286,155,734,497,1,PDFP
ReformVal: [Undefined,Null,Undefined,Undefined,1]
{!40000|Att_ResultSliceState: [Trade_direction,1,Sector,3,Sys_LocalIndex('Step'),1]}
{!40000|Att_XRole: -1}
{!40000|Att_ColorRole: Null}

Chance Domestic_equil_price
Title: Domestic equil price increase
Description: How much the cost of steel or Aluminum domestically will increase as a result of the tariffs in the long run.~
~
Our thinking is that initially domestic production capacity is insufficient to meet domestic demand, so the immediate impact would be an increase by roughly the amount of the tariff. But eventually, the industry will ramp up production because it will be profitable to do so, and domestic competition will drive prices to some new equilibrium that in between the starting point and the tariff amount. This captures where that new equilibrium would be. We're avoiding a complicated equilibrium model here, so this is intended to be a crude estimate.
Definition: Triangular(0, 75%, 100%) * Proposed_Tariff
