Variable Trade_price_increase
Title: Trade price increase
Description: The percent change import and export prices across each sector. By import price, we mean the price at which US consumers purchase imported goods. By export price, we mean the price at which international buyers purchase goods exported from the US. The values here reflect the percent change. ~
~
For goods not directly targeted by the tariff (i.e., everything other than Steel and Aluminum), we assume no change to import prices. We also assume that the export price is the same as the domestic price. We reason that obvious economic forces would tend to cause that to be true.~
~
For steel and alumimum, we assume the import price increases by the tariff amount. This assumption would be violated if a reduction of US imports alters the international market equilbrium, for example. Since the US accounts for only 8% of global imports, we feel the assumption is within the fidelity of this analysis. We assume the export price for steel and aluminum also matches the domestic price. Basic economics should tend to push those to equality in the absense of relatiatory tariffs, etc.
Definition: Table(Sector,Trade_direction)(~
Proposed_tariff[Material='Steel'],Domestic_equil_price[Material='Steel'],~
Proposed_tariff[Material='Aluminum'],Domestic_equil_price[Material='Aluminum'],~
0,Domestic_price_incre,~
0,Domestic_price_incre,~
0,Domestic_price_incre,~
0,Domestic_price_incre,~
0,Domestic_price_incre,~
0,Domestic_price_incre,~
0,Domestic_price_incre,~
0,Domestic_price_incre,~
0,Domestic_price_incre)
